L000048	\begin{document}
L000049	\title{Spontaneous Quantum Turbulence in a Newborn Bose-Einstein Condensate via the Kibble-Zurek Mechanism}
L000050	
L000051	\author{Seong-Ho Shinn\orcidlink{0000-0002-2041-5292}}
L000052	\email{seongho.shin@uni.lu}
L000053	\affiliation{Department of Physics and Materials Science, University of Luxembourg, L-1511 Luxembourg, Luxembourg}
L000054	
L000055	\author{Matteo~Massaro\orcidlink{0009-0004-0935-8022}}
L000056	\email{matteo.massaro@uni.lu}
L000057	\affiliation{Department of Physics and Materials Science, University  of Luxembourg, L-1511 Luxembourg, Luxembourg}
L000058	
L000059	\author{Mithun Thudiyangal\orcidlink{0000-0003-4341-6439}}
L000060	\affiliation{Center for Quantum Technologies and Complex Systems (CQTCS), Christ University, Bengaluru, Karnataka 560074, India}
L000061	\affiliation{Department of Physics and Electronics, Christ University, Bengaluru, Karnataka 560029, India}
L000062	
L000063	\author{Adolfo del Campo\orcidlink{0000-0003-2219-2851}}
L000064	\affiliation{Department of Physics and Materials Science, University of Luxembourg, L-1511 Luxembourg, Luxembourg}
L000065	\affiliation{Donostia International Physics Center, E-20018 San Sebasti\'an, Spain}
L000066	
L000067	\begin{abstract}
L000068	The Kibble-Zurek mechanism (KZM) predicts the spontaneous formation of topological defects in a continuous phase transition driven at a finite rate. We propose the generation of spontaneous quantum turbulence (SQT) via the KZM during Bose-Einstein condensation induced by a thermal quench.
L000069	 Using numerical simulations of the stochastic projected Gross-Pitaevskii equation in
L000070	two spatial dimensions, we describe the formation of a newborn Bose-Einstein condensate proliferated by quantum vortices. We establish the nonequilibrium universality of SQT through the Kibble-Zurek and Kolmogorov scaling of the incompressible kinetic energy.
L000071	\end{abstract}
L000072	
L000073	\maketitle
L000074	
L000075	Understanding turbulence is one of the long-standing unsolved problems in physics, with broad applications in biology, medicine, industry, and weather prediction \cite{Kolmogorov41_first, Frisch1995}. Feynman pioneered the idea of quantum turbulence (QT) as an analog in superfluids proliferated by a tangle of vortices \cite{Feynman1955}. With the development of technology to create, manipulate, and probe quantum fluids,
L000076	 the study of quantum turbulence emerged as a new research field at the frontiers of nonequilibrium physics of complex systems, arising in superfluid helium, Bose-Einstein condensates, polaritonic condensates, and neutron stars \cite{BerloffSvistunov02,Finne2003,KobayashiPRL2005,Tsubota08book,SALMAN2009,Tsatsos16,Salman2016,Seo17,Wheeler_2021}, with applications ranging from atomtronics to astrophysics.
L000077	
L000078	The turbulent regime in quantum fluids describes the complex, chaotic dynamics of interacting quantized vortices, which are topological defects. This definition is loose, and as we shall see, different criteria for its diagnosis have been put forward, including the scaling of the energy spectrum on the wave number, velocity autocorrelation functions, circulation statistics, etc. The situation is reminiscent of the use of various inequivalent definitions of quantum chaos, emphasizing complementary features brought out by a diversity of diagnostic tools (spectral statistics, semiclassical orbits, out-of-time order correlators, etc.) \cite{Nandy25}.
L000079	
L000080	QT was discovered in low-temperature physics studies of superfluid helium. However, the remarkable experimental progress in the mid-90s offered a new highly controllable and tunable platform for its exploration: Bose-Einstein condensates (BECs) of ultracold atomic gases \cite{Ueda10} and the closely related quantum fluids of light \cite{Carusotto13}. The experimental measurement of quantized vortices in BEC was crucial in probing their superfluid character.
L000081	A BEC is characterized
L000082	by a macroscopic wave function that acts as a complex-valued order parameter
L000083	and can be conveniently written in a polar decomposition $\Psi(\boldsymbol{r})=\sqrt{
L000084	\rho \left( \boldsymbol{r} \right)
L000085	}\ e^{i\theta(\boldsymbol{r})}$ \cite{pitaevskii2003bose}. A defining feature of a superfluid is that the circulation $\Gamma[C]=\oint_C \boldsymbol{v}\cdot d\boldsymbol{\ell}$ (that is, the contour integral of the velocity around a given contour $C$) is quantized as a result of the single-valued character of the macroscopic wave function and the relation between the superfluid velocity $\boldsymbol{v}$ (equivalently, the vorticity $\boldsymbol{\omega}=\nabla\times\boldsymbol{v}$) and the condensate phase, $\boldsymbol{v}(\boldsymbol{r})=\hbar\nabla\theta(\boldsymbol{r})/m$,
L000086	
L000087	where $m$ is the mass of the particle.
L000088	QT differs from its classical counterpart in that quantum vortices are true topological defects and thus more stable than eddies, having conserved and quantized circulation \cite{Tsubota13,Tsatsos16}.
L000089	
L000090	The phenomenology of turbulence varies significantly with the spatial dimension.
L000091	In three spatial dimensions (3D), turbulence is characterized by a cascade of energy from large to small scales, with eddies breaking into ever-decreasing whirlpools. By contrast, two-dimensional (2D) turbulence is governed by the presence of an additional invariant of motion known as the enstrophy, and gives rise to an inverse energy cascade, with energy flowing from small to large scales.
L000092	In the quantum case, the spatial dimensionality of the superfluid can be varied by tuning the anisotropy of the external confinement in BEC experiments.
L000093	Notably, in 2D
L000094	QT,
L000095	enstrophy may not be conserved due to vortex-antivortex annihilation, and a direct energy cascade can therefore appear, as observed in numerical simulations \cite{Numasato2010,Chesler13} and
L000096	BEC experiments \cite{Zhao25}.
L000097	In contrast, its conservation leads to the clustering of vortices in bigger patches,
L000098	a behavior predicted by Onsager and experimentally reported in
L000099	BECs
L000100	\cite{Gau19,Johnstone19}
L000101	and exciton-polariton superfluids \cite{Panico23}.
L000102	
L000103	\begin{figure*}[hptb]
L000104	    \centering
L000105	    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{density_phase_tq10_and_KZ_scaling_v12.pdf}
L000106	    \caption{
L000107	    Typical condensate density and phase profiles at equilibration time, together with the vortex-number scaling.
L000108	    Panel (a) shows the condensate density \(|\Psi_{\mathcal{C}}(\boldsymbol{r},t_{\textrm{eq}})|^{2}\) at equilibration time $t_{\textrm{eq}}$ following a quench of duration $\tau_{Q}=10$, while panel (b) displays the corresponding phase of $\Psi_{\mathcal{C}}$. Vortices with topological charge \(w=+1\) are marked by red crosses, and those with \(w=-1\) by circles; no vortices with \(\lvert w\rvert>1\) were observed. The main plot in panel (c) shows the
L000109	    vortex count
L000110	    \( n_v \)
L000111	    at \(t_{\textrm{eq}}\) versus quench time \(\tau_Q\), demonstrating the Kibble-Zurek power-law scaling for \(10\le\tau_Q\le1000\). The inset plots
L000112	   \( {t}_{\textrm{eq}} \)
L000113	    against \(\tau_Q\), where ${t}_{\textrm{eq}}$ is the equilibration time measured from the crossing of the critical point. Data are averaged over \(\mathcal{R}=1000\) realizations, with error bars indicating 1 standard deviation.
L000114	}
L000115	\label{fig:density_and_phase_psi_with_marked_vortices_and_KZ_scaling}
L000116	\end{figure*}
L000117	Several protocols are used to induce QT in a superfluid sample. They generally rely on continuous pumping of energy. A predominant approach involves sweeping a repulsive potential \cite{Kwon14,Reeves2022}. This procedure is often too violent, complicating the study of QT, as a result of the generation of a variety of excitations.
L000118	As an alternative, one may wonder whether QT can emerge spontaneously, as topological defects emerge across a continuous phase transition. We refer to the QT that arises through finite-time spontaneous symmetry breaking as {\it spontaneous quantum turbulence} (SQT). In this context, the Kibble-Zurek mechanism (KZM) constitutes a powerful paradigm for describing universal critical dynamics \cite{Kibble76a,Kibble76b,Zurek96a,Zurek96b,DZ14}. It does so by exploiting equilibrium properties such as the divergence of the correlation length $\xi=\xi_0/|\varepsilon|^{\nu}$ and the relaxation time $\tau=\tau_0/|\varepsilon|^{z\nu}$ in the neighborhood of the critical point \cite{DZ14}.
L000119	Here, $\nu$ and $z$ are the correlation-length and dynamic critical exponents, $\varepsilon = (\lambda - \lambda_c)/\lambda_c$ with $\lambda$ the control parameter (e.g., temperature) and $\lambda_c$ its critical value, while $\xi_0$ and $\tau_0$ are nonuniversal constants with dimensions of length and time, respectively.
L000120	For a linearized quench $\varepsilon=t/\tau_Q$, the KZM introduces the universal scaling of the nonequilibrium correlation length $\hat\xi$ with the quench time $\tau_Q$, i.e., $\hat\xi=\xi_0(\tau_Q/\tau_0)^{\nu/(1+z\nu)}$. For pointlike defects, such as solitons in a cigar-shaped BEC \cite{Damski_soliton_2010,Lamporesi13}, and vortices in a pancake BEC \cite{Weiler08,Chomaz15}, the KZM further predicts the formation of vortices at a density $\rho_{v}$ that scales universally with the quench time
L000121	as $\rho_v
L000122	\propto 1 / \hat{\xi}^2=\left( 1 / \xi_0^2 \right) \left( \tau_0 / \tau_Q \right)^{2 \nu/(1+z\nu)}$.
L000123	In addition, it dictates that the response of the system is delayed by the freeze-out timescale $\hat{t}=(\tau_0 \tau_Q^{z\nu})^{1/(1+z\nu)}$. In scenarios characterized by
L000124	symmetry breaking that lead to vortex formation, the KZM has been widely tested in theoretical studies \cite{DRP11,Chesler15,Liu2020,Zeng23,Thudiyangal24,Wheeler2025,Kirkby2025}, with several experiments also supporting its role in superfluid formation and the BEC transition \cite{Ruutu1996,Weiler08,Navon15,Shin19,KimShin22}.
L000125	
L000126	In this Letter, we establish and describe SQT in a newborn BEC prepared by a thermal quench in finite time. We establish the Kolmogorov scaling of the incompressible kinetic energy
L000127	spectrum and demonstrate its universal scaling for different values of the quench time when rescaled by the nonequilibrium correlation length predicted by the Kibble-Zurek mechanism.
L000128	
L000129	{\it Kibble-Zurek dynamics of the BEC transition---}
L000130	To describe the nonequilibrium condensate dynamics and the emergence of SQT, we consider the
L000131	2D stochastic projected Gross-Pitaevskii equation \cite{Gardiner2003,blakie2008dynamics,Rooney2012,Rooney2014,Bradley2015,McDonald2020}.
L000132	This formalism has been widely used to model the BEC transition, with numerous studies demonstrating its quantitative agreement with experimental observations \cite{Weiler08,Liu2020}.
L000133	Specifically, it describes the evolution of the low-energy coherent modes of a Bose gas via a complex order parameter $\Psi_{\mathcal{C}}$ which,
L000134	in dimensionless units used throughout the manuscript,
L000135	evolves according to
L000136	\begin{equation}\label{2D_dimensionless_SPGPE_main}
L000137	    d{\Psi_{\mathcal{C}}} = \mathcal{P}_{\mathcal{C}} \left[ -(i + \gamma) \left( H_{\textrm{sp}} + g |\Psi_{\mathcal{C}}|^{2} -\mu\right) \Psi_{\mathcal{C}} \, dt + d \eta \right],
L000138	\end{equation}
L000139	where $\mu$ is the chemical potential, $\gamma$ is the damping rate modeling energy dissipation due to the contact with the thermal bath, $g$ is the coupling strength of the delta-function interaction between bosons, and $d\eta$ is a complex Gaussian noise increment accounting for thermal fluctuations.
L000140	$H_{\textrm{sp}} = - \left( 1 / 2 \right) \nabla^{2} + V(\boldsymbol{r})$ denotes the single-particle Hamiltonian, with $V(\boldsymbol{r})$ the external trapping potential. In this work, we focus on a periodic homogeneous system and set $V(\boldsymbol{r})=0$. Further details of Eq.~(\ref{2D_dimensionless_SPGPE_main}) and its numerical implementation are provided in \cite{SM}.
L000141	
L000142	To drive the phase transition, we consider a linear quench of the chemical potential, $\mu(t) = \mu_i + \left( \mu_f - \mu_i \right)t/\tau_Q$, with $\mu_i$ and $\mu_f$ denoting its initial and final values, respectively.
L000143	This protocol drives the system through the critical point $\mu_c$ at a finite rate $\tau_Q$, resulting in the formation of a BEC populated with vortices. A vortex is characterized by a quantized circulation of the superfluid velocity,
L000144	$
L000145	\oint \boldsymbol{v}(\boldsymbol{r},t)\cdot d \boldsymbol{\ell}=
L000146	2 \pi
L000147	w
L000148	$, where $w\in\mathbb{Z}$ is the winding number.
L000149	The associated singularity of the velocity field enforces a vanishing condensate density at the vortex center, with the healing length determining the core size.
L000150	An example of a single realization of the newborn BEC is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:density_and_phase_psi_with_marked_vortices_and_KZ_scaling}, where vortices can be recognized by the depletion of the BEC density at their core.
L000151	The associated phase map indicates that only the values $w=\pm1$ are generated in the course of the BEC transition. This is consistent with the fact that higher winding numbers are energetically costly and unstable against decay \cite{Tsubota08}.
L000152	
L000153	Using the mean-field values of the critical exponents $\nu = 1/2$ and $z = 2$, the KZM predicts the scaling of the freeze-out time, which sets the timescale in which the growth of the order parameter lags behind the crossing of the critical point set by the quench of $\mu(t)$. The equilibration time,
L000154	when the growth of the number of bosons in BEC changes from exponential to linear \cite{Chesler15,Thudiyangal24,SM},
L000155	is proportional to the freeze-out time and scales universally as
L000156	$t_{\textrm{eq}} \propto \hat{t}\propto\tau_Q^{1/2}$.
L000157	This scaling is confirmed by the numerical results shown in the inset of Fig.~\ref{fig:density_and_phase_psi_with_marked_vortices_and_KZ_scaling} (c), where
L000158	$t_{\textrm{eq}} = ( 3.779 \pm 0.693 ) \tau_Q^{0.541 \pm 0.069}$.
L000159	At the equilibration time, the KZM further predicts that the
L000160	vortex
L000161	number scales as $n_v \propto \tau_Q^{-1/2}$, in agreement with the fit shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:density_and_phase_psi_with_marked_vortices_and_KZ_scaling} (c), where $
L000162	n_v = \left( 249.036 \pm 44.999 \right) \tau_Q^{-0.534 \pm 0.047}
L000163	$.
L000164	
L000165	Since we consider a 2D system, the role of the Berezinskii-Kosterlitz-Thouless (BKT) transition and the associated thermal vortices deserves discussion. Following the quasi-2D Bose gas experiment of Ref.~\cite{Chomaz15}, which demonstrated negligible BKT effects and verified KZM power laws, we work in the low-temperature limit (
L000166	of order 1 nK or less) to isolate the SQT arising from the symmetry-breaking phase transition.
L000167	In this regime,
L000168	the expected number of thermal vortices is negligible \cite{Giorgetti2007}. This is further supported by the time evolution of the vortex count shown in the Supplemental Material \cite{SM}: $n_v$ decays monotonically to zero, indicating that all observed vortices are of KZM origin rather than thermally activated, consistent with the experimental findings of Ref.~\cite{Chomaz15}.
L000169	
L000170	{\it Compressible and incompressible energy spectra---}
L000171	By introducing the density-weighted velocity field $\boldsymbol{u}(\boldsymbol{r})=|\Psi_{\mathcal{C}}(\boldsymbol{r})|\boldsymbol{v}(\boldsymbol{r})$ \cite{Nore1997PRL,Nore97,Ogawa2002,Kobayashi2005}, the kinetic energy of the newborn BEC takes the form
L000172	$
L000173	E=
L000174	\left(
L000175	1
L000176	/ 2 \right)
L000177	\int |\boldsymbol{u}(\boldsymbol{r})|^2 \, d^2 \boldsymbol{r} .
L000178	$
L000179	Using the canonical decomposition \cite{Nore1997PRL,Nore97,Ogawa2002,Kobayashi2005}, $\boldsymbol{u}$ can be separated into a compressible (irrotational) component $\boldsymbol{u}_c$, satisfying $\nabla \times \boldsymbol{u}_c = 0$, and an incompressible (solenoidal) component $\boldsymbol{u}_{i}$, for which $\nabla \cdot \boldsymbol{u}_{i} = 0$. The total kinetic energy is accordingly decomposed into its compressible and incompressible contributions,
L000180	\begin{equation}
L000181	E_{i,c} = \frac{
L000182	1
L000183	}{2} \int |\boldsymbol{u}_{i,c}(\boldsymbol{r})|^2 \, d^2 \boldsymbol{r}.
L000184	\label{eq:energy_cic}
L000185	\end{equation}
L000186	It is particularly useful to express Eq.~(\ref{eq:energy_cic}) in Fourier space, resulting in
L000187	$E_{i,c} = \int E_{i,c}(k) \, d k$, with
L000188	\begin{equation}
L000189	E_{i,c}(k) \coloneqq
L000190	\frac{k}{2}
L000191	\int
L000192	|\boldsymbol{\tilde{u}}_{i, c} ( \boldsymbol{k} )|^{2} \, d \varphi_k,
L000193	\label{eq:energy_cic_fft}
L000194	\end{equation}
L000195	where $\boldsymbol{k} = k ( \cos \varphi_k, \sin \varphi_k )$ and $\boldsymbol{\tilde{u}}_{i,c}(\boldsymbol{k})$ is the Fourier transform of $\boldsymbol{u}_{i,c}(\boldsymbol{r})$ \cite{Bradley12}.
L000196	
L000197	{\it Kolmogorov scaling of the energy spectrum in SQT---}
L000198	The occurrence of SQT in the newborn BEC is diagnosed by the Kolmogorov scaling of the incompressible kinetic energy spectrum
L000199	$E_i \left( k \right)$ \cite{Tsubota13,Neely13}, which has been experimentally measured in 2D BECs \cite{Zhao25}.
L000200	This law was originally derived for homogeneous isotropic turbulence where large eddies (with high Reynolds number) are continuously broken into smaller eddies (with small Reynolds number), and there exists a range of length scales (inertial range) where the details of energy injection and dissipation can be neglected and the eddies follow universal statistical properties \cite{Frisch1995,Barenghi2023textbook}.
L000201	With the additional assumptions that, within this regime,
L000202	the change of the kinetic energy per unit time
L000203	is independent of $k$ and the viscosity does not affect $E_i(k)$,
L000204	dimensional arguments imply a $k^{-5/3}$ scaling of the incompressible kinetic energy spectrum, independent of spatial dimensionality \cite{Vinen02,Barenghi2023textbook}.
L000205	This universal law
L000206	is a direct reflection of the velocity-velocity autocorrelation function \cite{Kolmogorov41,Frisch1995}.
L000207	At higher momenta,
L000208	the
L000209	inertial range
L000210	over which the Kolmogorov scaling extends is interrupted by a second power law, $E_i(k) \propto k^{-3}$, that reflects the short-range properties of the vortex core and is thus unrelated to turbulence \cite{Bradley12,Tanogami21}.
L000211	As the radius of the vortex core is of the order of the condensate healing length $
L000212	\xi_h := \sqrt{
L000213	1 / (2
L000214	\mu_f)}
L000215	$ \cite{ginzburg1958,pitaevskii2003bose,Tanogami21}, the inertial range lies below $k < 2 \pi / \xi_h$.
L000216	At large scales, it is further bounded by the mean intervortex distance $l_{v}$ \cite{Bradley12}. The width of this range is therefore determined by the quench speed, which sets the energy-injection scale through the universal Kibble-Zurek (KZ) correlation length $\hat{\xi} \sim l_{v}$.
L000217	As a result, the extent of the Kolmogorov scaling shrinks for faster quenches and broadens for moderate ones. \begin{figure}[ptb]
L000218	\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{QT_E_i_E_c_vs_k_v9_100_350.pdf}
L000219	\caption{
L000220	Incompressible and compressible kinetic energy spectra.
L000221	Panels (a) and (b) show the incompressible kinetic energy spectrum $E_{i}(k)$ at the equilibration time following quenches with
L000222	$\tau_{Q}=100$ and $\tau_{Q}=350$,
L000223	respectively, each averaged over $\mathcal{R}=1000$ independent noise realizations. The corresponding fitted power-law exponents are
L000224	$\alpha = 1.710 \pm 0.079$, $\beta = 3.025 \pm 0.030$ for (a), and
L000225	$\alpha = 1.654 \pm 0.031$, $\beta = 3.036 \pm 0.047$
L000226	for (b). Panels (c) and (d) display the compressible spectrum $E_{c}(k)$ for the same quench protocols, with fit parameters
L000227	$(\eta, \beta)=(0.992 \pm 0.008, 3.090 \pm 0.110)$ and
L000228	$(1.002 \pm 0.008, 3.111 \pm 0.223)$
L000229	respectively.
L000230	The vertical dashed lines mark $k = 2\pi / d_v$, where $d_v = 4 \xi_h$ gives a good estimate of the vortex diameter in our system,
L000231	while the vertical dotted lines indicate $k = 2 \pi / l_v$, with $l_v$ being the mean nearest intervortex distance. Shaded error bands correspond to 1 standard deviation.
L000232	}
L000233	\label{fig:E_vs_k}
L000234	\end{figure}
L000235	This effect is illustrated in Fig. \ref{fig:E_vs_k}, showing the incompressible energy spectra at equilibration for quenches of duration $\tau_{Q}=100$ in (a) and $\tau_{Q}=350$ in (b).
L000236	The corresponding fits yield $E_i \left( k \right) = \left( 176.555 \pm 14.316 \right) k^{-1.710 \pm 0.079}$, $E_i \left( k \right) = \left( 1028.257 \pm 123.069 \right) k^{-3.025 \pm 0.030}$ in panel (a), and $E_i \left( k \right) = \left( 37.722 \pm 1.723 \right) k^{-1.654 \pm 0.031}$, $E_i \left( k \right) = \left( 226.968 \pm 42.383 \right) k^{-3.036 \pm 0.047}$ in (b),
L000237	where in each panel the first and second expressions refer to the red dashed and black dotted lines, respectively.
L000238	The power-law fitted exponents in the inertial range
L000239	are thus in agreement with the Kolmogorov scaling value $5/3\simeq 1.67$ within the numerical uncertainty.
L000240	In
L000241	BECs the inertial range is typically narrow, spanning no more than one decade due to the lack of a wide separation between characteristic length scales \cite{Kobayashi2005, Tsubota13, Barenghi2023textbook}.
L000242	This limitation can be mitigated by analyzing the velocity structure functions, which, due to their self-similar nature, allow the extension of the Kolmogorov scaling beyond the inertial range.
L000243	In particular, in \cite{SM} we compute the longitudinal velocity increments $S_p(r)=\langle |[\boldsymbol{u}_i(\boldsymbol{R}+\boldsymbol{r})-\boldsymbol{u}_i(\boldsymbol{R})]\cdot
L000244	\boldsymbol{r} / r
L000245	|^p \rangle$ and find clear power-law relations $S_p(r)\propto [S_3(r)]^{\zeta(p)}$, with exponents that deviate from the K41 prediction $\zeta(p)=p/3$
L000246	\cite{Kolmogorov41_first,Kolmogorov41,Benzi1993,Dubrulle1994,Ciliberto1994,Frisch1995,Krstulovic2016,Barenghi2023textbook,Zhao25}, and are more accurately described by the refined K62 model \cite{Kolmogorov_1962}.
L000247	While the KZM accurately describes the universal critical dynamics across the BEC transition, it does not account for other effects such as the annihilation of vortex-antivortex pairs, the losses of vortices at the edges of the trap, and coarsening.
L000248	For slow quenches, the system evolves nearly adiabatically, resulting in suppressed vortex formation and diminished turbulence. As a consequence, the inertial range shrinks and the Kolmogorov scaling becomes less pronounced.
L000249	
L000250	Although our main interest lies in the Kolmogorov scaling of the incompressible kinetic energy spectrum, we also plotted $E_c(k)$ at the equilibration time in Figs. \ref{fig:E_vs_k}(c) and \ref{fig:E_vs_k}(d).
L000251	For a 2D system at thermodynamic equilibrium, it is reported that $E_c(k) \propto k$ for small $k$ \cite{Numasato2010}.
L000252	In addition, since $E(k)$ follows the $k^{-3}$ law at large $k$ \cite{Nore97,Krstulovic10}, $E_c(k)$ is expected to follow the same power law. Our numerical results in Figs. \ref{fig:E_vs_k}(c) and \ref{fig:E_vs_k}(d) are in agreement with these expectations.
L000253	\begin{figure}[ptb]
L000254	\includegraphics[width=0.8\columnwidth]{KZ_scaling_E_i_k_Kolmogorov_v10.pdf}
L000255	\caption{
L000256	Kibble-Zurek universality of the incompressible kinetic energy spectrum.
L000257	Panel (a) shows $E_i \left( k \right)$ at equilibration time for various $\tau_{Q}$ values, each averaged over $\mathcal{R}=1000$ stochastic realizations.
L000258	The
L000259	dashed
L000260	vertical line marks
L000261	$k = 2\pi / d_v$ with the vortex diameter estimated to be $d_v = 4 \xi_h$.
L000262	Panel (b) displays
L000263	$\left\langle E_i \left( k \right) \right\rangle \tau_Q^{
L000264	3/4
L000265	}
L000266	$
L000267	as a function of the scaled momentum $k \tau_Q^{1/4} \propto k \hat{\xi}$.
