L000484	\subsection{Determination of the equilibration time}
L000485	The equilibration time $t_{\textrm{eq}}$ is identified by analyzing the time evolution of the norm of the order parameter, $N_{\mathcal{C}}/L^{2} =(1/L^{2})
L000486	\int |\Psi_{\mathcal{C}}(\boldsymbol{r})|^{2} \, d^{2}\boldsymbol{r}$.
L000487	In the spirit of \cite{Chesler15}, we locate the global maximum of $d^{2}(N_{\mathcal{C}}/L^2)/dt^{2}$ and identify the subsequent interval $(t_1, t_2)$ where $d^{2}(N_{\mathcal{C}}/L^2)/dt^{2} \le -\Delta$, with $\Delta$ set to $10\%$ of the maximum. We then take $t_{\textrm{eq}}=t_{2}$. The procedure is illustrated in Fig. \ref{fig:NC_sec_NC_teq}.
L000488	
L000489	The equilibration time measured from the critical crossing scales with the Kibble-Zurek (KZ) freeze-out time as
L000490	\begin{equation}
L000491	\bar{t}_{\mathrm{eq}} \coloneqq t_{\mathrm{eq}} - t_c \propto \hat{t} \propto \tau_Q^{z \nu /(1 + z \nu)},
L000492	\end{equation}
L000493	where \(t_c = \tau_Q (\mu_c - \mu_i) / (\mu_f - \mu_i)\) denotes the time at which the system crosses the critical point, and \(\mu_c \coloneqq \mu(t_c)\) is the corresponding value of the chemical potential.
L000494	This universal scaling is confirmed in the mean-field regime by the numerical results presented in the main text, which yield
L000495	\begin{equation}
L000496	t_{\mathrm{eq}} = (3.779 \pm 0.693)\,\tau_Q^{0.541 \pm 0.069} + (0.078 \pm 0.059)\,\tau_Q.
L000497	\end{equation}
L000498	
